  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "At a fair, a carousel makes $3$ revolutions in  $\\frac{3}{5}$ minutes. How many revolutions does it make in $1$ minute?",
  "worked_solution": "To find out the number of revolutions the carousel makes in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of revolutions per minute. We do this by dividing the number of revolutions, $3,$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{5}$, as follows:  \n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{3}{5} & =3\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the carousel makes  $5$ revolutions in $1$ minute.",
